<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Staffordshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Staffordshire is a county in the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> as its county town. The area lay in the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Mercia, whose bishopric was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> and whose wealth is reflected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard">Staffordshire Hoard</a> found in 2009. In the industrial era the Potteries around Stoke-on-Trent made it the centre of English ceramics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/staffordshire.jpg" alt="Staffordshire" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Rayner · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoke-on-Trent_Bottle_Kiln_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6995.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mercia, from the Old English Mierce, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper Trent valley, around what later became <b>Staffordshire</b>, Derbyshire and Leicestershire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Blore Heath in <b>Staffordshire</b>, near Market Drayton, the Yorkists won a battle of the Wars of the Roses, the struggle between the houses of Lancaster and York for the English throne that lasted until 1487.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cornovii occupied Shropshire, Cheshire and parts of <b>Staffordshire</b> and the Welsh borderland in the late Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li></ul></section>
